mode: "100644"
text: "\n# Video Editing Principles Master Reference (2026 Edition)\n\nProfessional video editing principles, techniques, and workflows synthesized from American Cinema Editors research, ITU-R BS.1770 standards, Walter Murch's editorial theory, W3C accessibility guidelines, and production post-production standards.\n\n---\n\n## Walter Murch's Rule of Six (Editorial Hierarchy)\n\nWhen evaluating any potential cut, prioritize criteria in this descending order:\n\n| Priority | Dimension | Practical Objective |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Emotion** (51%) | Does the edit preserve and transmit the intended feeling? |\n| 2 | **Story Advancement** (23%) | Does the cut advance the narrative or provide new information? |\n| 3 | **Rhythm** (10%) | Does the edit land at a point of musical or physical cadence? |\n| 4 | **Eye-Trace** (7%) | Does the audience's focus area transfer smoothly to the next composition? |\n| 5 | **Screen-Plane Continuity** (5%) | Do the 2D visual vectors and screen positions remain comprehensible? |\n| 6 | **3D Spatial Continuity** (4%) | Does the edit preserve accurate physical geography in 3D space? |\n\n*Never sacrifice higher-ranking dimensions (Emotion, Story, Rhythm) merely to achieve spatial or continuity perfection.*\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — Fundamental Editing Philosophy\n\n### 1. Essential Decision-Making Principles\n- **Emotion First**: Choose edits that preserve the intended feeling.\n- **Story Advancement**: Every cut must add narrative value or context.\n- **Rhythm**: Cut when the physical or conversational moment demands it.\n- **Eye-Trace**: Guide the audience's visual attention across frame transitions.\n- **Screen-Plane Continuity**: Maintain clear 2D spatial relationships.\n- **Spatial Continuity**: Preserve scene geography unless disorientation is purposeful.\n- **Intentionality**: Every edit must serve a defined objective.\n- **Clarity Over Complexity**: Prioritize message clarity over technical display.\n- **Invisible Editing**: Avoid drawing attention to the cut mechanism unless stylistically motivated.\n- **Show, Don't Just Tell**: Present visual evidence rather than mere spoken assertion.\n- **Economy of Storytelling**: Remove frames that add zero new value.\n- **Editorial Restraint**: Reject decorative effects that weaken visual weight.\n- **Visual Consistency**: Maintain a coherent visual and color treatment.\n- **Emotional Continuity**: Preserve believable feeling and psychological state across shots.\n- **Audience-First Thinking**: Edit for the viewer's cognitive and emotional load.\n- **Motivated Cutting**: Cut only when a meaningful visual, narrative, or audio change occurs.\n- **Purposeful Rule-Breaking**: Subvert conventions deliberately when it strengthens emotional delivery.\n- **Subtraction Principle**: Improve a sequence by removing weak frames rather than adding more material.\n- **Clarity of Intention**: Define what each scene must achieve before assembling cuts.\n- **Creative Authorship**: Maintain human artistic responsibility over narrative structure.\n\n### 2. Storytelling and Narrative Structure\n- **Three-Act Structure**: Setup, confrontation, resolution.\n- **Five-Act Structure**: Granular dramatic progression through exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, and denouement.\n- **Hero's Journey**: Transformation-driven narrative progression.\n- **Setup and Payoff**: Plant narrative details that resolve with consequence later.\n- **Cause and Effect**: Chain scenes through direct causality rather than arbitrary sequence.\n- **Open Loops**: Raise unresolved questions early to command forward attention.\n- **Narrative Tension**: Maintain calibrated uncertainty regarding outcomes.\n- **Escalating Stakes**: Intensify consequences as narrative progresses.\n- **Conflict Progression**: Transform the nature of the core obstacle over time.\n- **Character Arc**: Visually track internal character changes across decisions.\n- **Emotional Arc**: Shape the cadence of peaks, troughs, and releases.\n- **Narrative Compression**: Transmit complex concepts through condensed visual shorthand.\n- **Cold Open**: Begin directly with high-impact material before establishing credits.\n- **In Medias Res**: Launch the narrative inside an active conflict.\n- **Foreshadowing**: Plant subtle visual or auditory hints of future developments.\n- **Revelation Timing**: Control the exact frame where critical knowledge becomes visible.\n- **Suspense Construction**: Prolong anticipation before delivering a known outcome.\n- **Surprise Construction**: Conceal critical information until an immediate reveal.\n- **Dramatic Irony**: Give the audience information that the on-screen subject lacks.\n- **Parallel Storytelling**: Advance multiple distinct plotlines concurrently.\n- **Nonlinear Storytelling**: Reorder chronological time to emphasize conceptual connections.\n- **Circular Storytelling**: Conclude by returning to the visual motif of the opening frame.\n- **Visual Symbolism**: Use recurring objects and lighting to reinforce core themes.\n- **Motif Repetition**: Repeat visual framing or sound cues to indicate thematic recurrence.\n- **Thematic Coherence**: Align color, cutting tempo, and performance with the central message.\n- **Narrative Perspective**: Anchor the viewer's experience through a clear point of view.\n- **Information Hierarchy**: Deliver primary facts before secondary supporting data.\n- **Ending Resonance**: Conclude with emotional resolution or intellectual impact.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 — Cinematic Editing Techniques\n\n### 3. Performance and Dialogue Editing\n- **Performance Selection**: Select the most truthful, grounded take over technical perfection.\n- **Reaction-First Editing**: Cut to the listener when their reaction carries more weight than the speaker's lines.\n- **Micro-Expression Timing**: Preserve brief facial movements that reveal underlying emotion.\n- **Dialogue Overlap**: Create natural cadence by overlapping trailing and leading words across cuts.\n- **Conversational Rhythm**: Respect the natural breath and cadence of authentic human dialogue.\n- **Breath Preservation**: Retain natural inhalation before emotionally charged statements.\n- **Intentional Pauses**: Let silence indicate contemplation or emotional gravity.\n- **Subtext Editing**: Cut to reveal what is unsaid beneath spoken words.\n- **Emotional Reaction Delay**: Allow sufficient frames for dramatic impact to register on faces.\n- **Listening-Shot Selection**: Show how dialogue lands on the recipient.\n- **Performance Continuity**: Match intensity and vocal energy across shot changes.\n- **Take Blending**: Splice the best portions of multiple takes into one unified performance.\n- **Dialogue Cleanup**: Eliminate distracting verbal tics without rendering speech robotic.\n- **Point-of-View Editing**: Cut from a character's gaze directly to their subjective field of view.\n- **Eye-Contact Timing**: Calibrate the duration of mutual gaze to control perceived intimacy or tension.\n\n### 4. Continuity and Spatial Editing\n- **180-Degree Rule**: Maintain the established axis of action to preserve left-right screen direction.\n- **30-Degree Rule**: Shift camera angle by at least 30 degrees or change focal length when cutting to avoid jarring jump cuts.\n- **Eyeline Match**: Align a character's gaze angle with the placement of their conversational partner.\n- **Match on Action**: Cut during active physical movement to mask the transition point.\n- **Screen Direction**: Maintain consistent left-to-right or right-to-left travel across cuts.\n- **Shot-Reverse-Shot**: Intercut complementary over-the-shoulder or single views for dialogue.\n- **Establishing Shot**: Orient viewers within physical space at scene opening.\n- **Re-Establishing Shot**: Restore spatial geography after extended close-up coverage.\n- **Master-Shot Coverage**: Anchor complex blocking using a wide reference perspective.\n- **Insert Shots**: Isolate and magnify critical story props or environmental details.\n- **Cutaways**: Shift briefly to related secondary action to manage pacing or compress time.\n- **Action Continuity**: Align physical body posture and object interaction across cuts.\n- **Prop Continuity**: Ensure handheld objects remain in matching positions between shots.\n- **Wardrobe Continuity**: Guard against accidental costume shifts between takes.\n- **Lighting Continuity**: Match direction, color temperature, and contrast ratio across angles.\n- **Temporal Continuity**: Preserve logical chronological progression unless explicitly compressed.\n- **Spatial Geography**: Anchor the physical position of all subjects relative to room boundaries.\n- **Movement Vector Matching**: Align the direction and velocity of moving subjects across cuts.\n- **Headroom Continuity**: Maintain consistent vertical headroom across alternating character singles.\n- **Look-Room Consistency**: Provide negative space in the direction of the subject's gaze.\n- **Intentional Discontinuity**: Subvert spatial rules deliberately to induce disorientation or shock.\n\n### 5. Types of Cuts and Transitions\n- **Straight Cut / Hard Cut**: Direct, unadorned transition between two shots.\n- **Jump Cut**: Cut between two shots of the same subject with negligible angle change, skipping time.\n- **Match Cut**: Transition between distinct scenes connected through visual, sonic, or conceptual similarity.\n- **Graphic Match**: Align shapes, geometry, or colors across the cut boundary.\n- **Match on Action**: Splice during an ongoing movement to conceal the edit point.\n- **Smash Cut**: Abrupt, high-contrast shift between quiet and loud, or dark and bright scenes.\n- **J-Cut**: Audio of the incoming scene precedes the visual cut.\n- **L-Cut**: Audio of the outgoing scene continues beneath the incoming visual.\n- **Split Edit**: Separate the audio and video edit points to maintain conversational flow.\n- **Cross-Cutting**: Alternate between simultaneous actions happening in different locations.\n- **Parallel Editing**: Interweave distinct narrative threads to draw thematic comparison.\n- **Cut on Motion**: Use subject or camera movement to motivate and hide the transition.\n- **Cut on Gaze**: Transition from an observing character to what they are inspecting.\n- **Cut on Sound**: Anchor the cut boundary directly to an audio event or transient.\n- **Cut on Musical Beat**: Align cut points with rhythmic musical pulses.\n- **Cut Against the Beat**: Place cuts slightly off-beat to generate tension or urgency.\n- **Reaction Cut**: Prioritize the respondent's reaction over the initiating event.\n- **Cutaway Transition**: Bridge two separate scenes using an intervening neutral shot.\n- **Invisible Cut**: Disguise an edit within rapid camera pans, dark occlusions, or foreground passes.\n- **Whip-Pan Transition**: Connect scenes through rapid horizontal camera motion blur.\n- **Occlusion Cut**: Cut when a foreground object completely covers the camera lens.\n- **Dissolve / Cross-Dissolve**: Superimpose the tail of one shot over the head of the next to indicate time passage.\n- **Fade In / Fade Out**: Transition between footage and solid black or white frames.\n- **Dip to Black / White**: Briefly pass through black or white to punctuate scene chapters.\n- **Wipe**: Replace one image with another across a traveling spatial line.\n- **Iris Transition**: Open or close a circular mask to isolate a subject.\n- **Morph Transition**: Interpolate between two similar compositions using geometric warping.\n\n### 6. Pacing, Rhythm, Montage, and Time\n- **Shot-Duration Control**: Modulate the on-screen duration of each shot to control reading time.\n- **Pacing Variation**: Alternate between rapid-fire sequences and sustained observational holds.\n- **Rhythmic Contrast**: Juxtapose fast and slow tempos to reset viewer attention.\n- **Internal Rhythm**: Pace cuts in response to physical action and camera movement within the frame.\n- **External Rhythm**: Generate cadence purely through the mathematical duration and placement of cuts.\n- **Emotional Pacing**: Lengthen duration to deepen pathos; shorten duration to increase adrenaline.\n- **Tension-Release Rhythm**: Alternate suspense buildup with moments of release.\n- **Metric Montage**: Edit according to strict frame duration regardless of scene content.\n- **Rhythmic Montage**: Balance physical movement within the shot with cut frequency.\n- **Tonal Montage**: Edit according to the atmospheric and emotional texture of the imagery.\n- **Overtonal Montage**: Synthesize metric, rhythmic, and tonal dynamics simultaneously.\n- **Intellectual Montage**: Juxtapose two unrelated images to create a third intellectual concept.\n- **Kuleshov Effect**: Demonstrate that viewers derive more meaning from the interaction of two sequential shots than from a single shot in isolation.\n- **Associative Montage**: Connect disparate visual concepts through thematic association.\n- **Time Compression**: Omit non-essential intervals to advance action efficiently.\n- **Time Expansion**: Prolong a brief event across multiple overlapping camera perspectives.\n- **Elliptical Editing**: Omit expected intermediate steps, trusting the viewer's inference.\n- **Slow Motion**: Decelerate speed to examine detail, express memory, or heighten dramatic impact.\n- **Fast Motion**: Accelerate footage to create comedic urgency or compress prolonged processes.\n- **Speed Ramping**: Shift smoothly between normal, slow, and fast speeds within a single shot.\n- **Freeze Frame**: Halt motion on a specific frame to emphasize an outcome or register a character.\n- **Flashback Editing**: Insert historical events to provide immediate motivation or context.\n- **Flash-Forward Editing**: Insert brief glimpses of future events to provoke curiosity.\n- **Long-Take Preservation**: Maintain unbroken shots when continuous performance is more powerful than cutting.\n\n### 7. Composition and Visual Attention\n- **Rule of Thirds**: Position focal subjects along the 3x3 compositional grid intersections.\n- **Central Framing**: Position subjects at center to convey authority, confrontation, or direct intimacy.\n- **Symmetry**: Construct formal balance to project control or artificial order.\n- **Asymmetry**: Unbalance the frame deliberately to generate psychological tension.\n- **Leading Lines**: Use architectural or environmental geometry to channel the eye to the focal subject.\n- **Negative Space**: Provide empty areas to communicate isolation, scale, or breathing room.\n- **Visual Hierarchy**: Structure brightness, scale, and focus so primary elements are seen first.\n- **Figure-Ground Separation**: Isolate the subject clearly from the background plane.\n- **Depth Layering**: Construct depth using foreground framing, middle-ground subject, and background context.\n- **Color Contrast**: Direct attention through contrasting complementary color palettes.\n- **Brightness Contrast**: Guide gaze toward the brightest point of interest in the composition.\n- **Scale Contrast**: Use juxtaposed size differences to establish dominance or vulnerability.\n- **Shot-Size Progression**: Transition logically through wide, medium, close-up, and extreme close-up.\n- **Visual Balance**: Distribute visual mass across the frame to avoid unintentional lopsidedness.\n- **Eye-Trace Matching**: Align the focal exit point of shot A with the entry point of shot B.\n- **Motion Continuity**: Preserve directional vector momentum between cuts.\n- **Visual Breathing Room**: Allot complex, wide shots sufficient duration for visual exploration.\n- **Silhouette Readability**: Ensure key characters and props remain instantly recognizable in silhouette.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 — Sound, Music, Color, and Visual Effects\n\n### 8. Professional Sound Editing and Design\n- **Dialogue Intelligibility**: Ensure speech remains crisp, present, and completely understandable.\n- **Room-Tone Continuity**: Bridge dialogue edits with continuous environmental room tone beds.\n- **Ambient Sound Beds**: Establish setting, weather, and world scale using layered background textures.\n- **Foley**: Recreate synchronized physical contact sounds (footsteps, cloth movement, prop handling).\n- **Diegetic Sound**: Employ audio originating directly from the world inside the frame.\n- **Non-Diegetic Sound**: Employ score, voiceover, and abstract sound design outside the story world.\n- **Sound Bridges**: Use sound to span across scenes, stitching distinct locations together.\n- **Audio Prelap (J-Cut)**: Introduce incoming audio before visual transition.\n- **Audio Postlap (L-Cut)**: Extend outgoing audio past the visual transition.\n- **Automated Dialogue Replacement (ADR)**: Re-record dialogue under clean studio conditions.\n- **Audio Synchronization**: Lock audio transients to on-screen contact frames with sub-frame accuracy.\n- **Equalization (EQ)**: Carve out frequencies to clean muddiness and give each element its sonic space.\n- **Noise Reduction**: Remove hiss, hum, and broadband noise without introducing phase artifacts.\n- **De-Reverberation**: Attenuate room reflections from poorly treated location recordings.\n- **De-Essing**: Tame harsh sibilance in dialogue tracks (4kHz - 8kHz).\n- **Dynamic Compression**: Control dynamic range to ensure even dialogue audibility.\n- **Expansion / Noise Gating**: Silence background noise floor during pauses in speech.\n- **Peak Limiting**: Guard against digital clipping at 0 dBFS.\n- **Volume Automation**: Ride dialogue, music, and effects faders dynamically shot by shot.\n- **Sidechain Ducking**: Automatically attenuate music beds by 12dB to 18dB during voiceover.\n- **Stereo Imaging**: Place sound sources across the left-right acoustic spectrum to match visual positions.\n- **Spatial Audio**: Position sounds in three-dimensional binaural or surround space.\n- **Reverb Design**: Match acoustic space (size, early reflections, decay) to on-screen environments.\n- **Sound Perspective**: Scale volume and high-frequency content to match camera distance from the subject.\n- **Transient Shaping**: Enhance punch on sound effects and soften harsh vocal transients.\n- **Loudness Normalization**: Master audio to destination standards (YouTube: -14 LUFS, Broadcast: -24 LUFS).\n- **Audio Stem Separation**: Deliver distinct tracks for dialogue (DX), music (MX), and sound effects (FX).\n\n### 9. Music Editing and Synchronization\n- **Tempo Mapping**: Map musical beats and measures to timeline frames.\n- **Beat Alignment**: Align critical cuts with downbeats and rhythmic accents.\n- **Musical Phrase Cutting**: Cut at the resolution of musical sentences rather than arbitrary mid-bar points.\n- **Syncopated Editing**: Place cuts on syncopated upbeats to create driving energetic flow.\n- **Energy Matching**: Calibrate scene editing velocity to match the emotional intensity of the score.\n- **Impact Synchronization**: Hit major visual story moments on musical swells and orchestral accents.\n- **Crescendo Building**: Construct rising edit density to match swelling musical arrangements.\n- **Drop Synchronization**: Align major visual reveals or action climaxes with musical drops.\n- **Strategic Silence**: Cut all music completely to induce sudden tension or highlight dialogue weight.\n- **Musical Crossfading**: Blend tracks across shared harmonic keys or percussion bridges.\n- **Music Licensing**: Clear commercial synchronization and master rights before release.\n- **Source Music Integration**: Blend diegetic music (radios, clubs) into non-diegetic score.\n- **Underscore Placement**: Support narrative subtext without competing with dialogue frequencies.\n- **Musical Stingers**: Deploy short musical punctuations to emphasize comedic or dramatic beats.\n\n### 10. Professional Color Correction and Grading\n- **Color-Managed Workflow**: Enforce consistent color science (ACES, DaVinci YRGB) from capture to deliverable.\n- **White-Balance Correction**: Neutralize unwanted color casts using neutral white and gray targets.\n- **Exposure Correction**: Normalize exposure values to establish proper shadow and highlight boundaries.\n- **Waveform Analysis**: Evaluate luminance distribution across IRE levels.\n- **Vectorscope Analysis**: Monitor chroma saturation and verify skin tones against the skin-tone indicator line.\n- **RGB Parade Analysis**: Balance individual red, green, and blue color channels across tonal zones.\n- **Black-Point Control**: Set shadow density to prevent washed-out blacks or crushed shadow detail.\n- **White-Point Control**: Anchor highlights to retain detail without clipping.\n- **Contrast Shaping**: Mold the S-curve to establish photographic depth.\n- **Gamma / Midtone Adjustment**: Lift or lower midtones to preserve facial visibility without shifting endpoints.\n- **Saturation Control**: Regulate chroma intensity to maintain natural color harmony.\n- **Hue Correction**: Fine-tune individual color wavelengths to match brand or natural palettes.\n- **Shot Matching**: Balance consecutive shots so contrast, exposure, and color temperature match consistently.\n- **Secondary Color Grading**: Isolate specific hues (e.g. skies, foliage) for targeted adjustment.\n- **Tracked Grading Masks**: Anchor localized color grades to moving faces or objects using point/cloud trackers.\n- **Skin-Tone Consistency**: Preserve natural, healthy complexion values across lighting setups.\n- **Highlight Roll-Off**: Engineer smooth, filmic highlight rolloff rather than harsh digital clipping.\n- **Gamut Management**: Constrain color values within target color spaces (Rec.709, DCI-P3, Rec.2020).\n- **Log-to-Display Transforms**: Apply accurate mathematical conversions from camera log to target display.\n- **Technical vs. Creative LUTs**: Distinguish between utility transforms and stylistic creative looks.\n- **SDR Mastering**: Grade within standard dynamic range limits (100 nits, Rec.709).\n- **HDR Mastering**: Grade for high dynamic range displays (1000 nits, PQ/HLG, Rec.2020).\n- **Film-Grain Treatment**: Apply authentic, size-appropriate grain texture to unify composite elements.\n- **Look Consistency and Restraint**: Avoid aggressive grading changes that distract from story performance.\n\n### 11. Motion Graphics, Animation, and VFX Principles\n- **Keyframe Animation**: Animate spatial, rotation, and scale parameters across time.\n- **Easing Curves**: Shape cubic-bezier velocity curves to ensure organic acceleration and deceleration.\n- **Squash and Stretch**: Convey weight, impact, and material elasticity.\n- **Anticipation**: Telegraph upcoming physical motion through subtle reverse preparation.\n- **Staging**: Direct attention to the primary animated event without background competition.\n- **Pose-to-Pose Animation**: Block key extreme poses before interpolating in-between frames.\n- **Straight-Ahead Animation**: Animate frame by frame to produce spontaneous, fluid movement.\n- **Follow-Through**: Continue secondary appendages after the main body halts.\n- **Overlapping Action**: Offset different parts of an object to move at varying rates.\n- **Slow In and Slow Out**: Soften arrivals and departures through exponential ease curves.\n- **Arcs**: Guide all natural motion along curved trajectories rather than rigid straight vectors.\n- **Secondary Action**: Add subordinate motion (hair sway, blinking) to reinforce main action.\n- **Animation Timing**: Calibrate duration to establish mass, scale, and emotional intention.\n- **Exaggeration**: Amplify motion characteristics to enhance readability and personality.\n- **Solid Form / Drawing**: Preserve structural volume and three-dimensional solidity during movement.\n- **Visual Appeal**: Design shapes and movements that are pleasing and readable.\n- **Motion Blur**: Simulate optical shutter exposure over motion to eliminate stuttering.\n- **Parallax**: Shift foreground, midground, and background layers at differential speeds to project depth.\n- **2.5D Composition**: Extrude flat 2D graphics into virtual 3D camera space.\n- **Camera Tracking**: Reconstruct 3D camera position and focal length from 2D footage for asset placement.\n- **Object Tracking**: Track planar or non-rigid moving surfaces to anchor graphics or replacements.\n- **Rotoscoping**: Trace animated vector masks to isolate foreground elements frame by frame.\n- **Chroma Keying**: Pull clean alpha mattes from green or blue screens with spill suppression.\n- **Compositing**: Combine live-action, CGI, and graphic layers into a unified image.\n- **Edge Blending**: Match edge softness, light wraps, and motion blur on composite boundaries.\n- **Light and Shadow Matching**: Replicate scene lighting direction, color, and shadow softness on added elements.\n- **Perspective Matching**: Align artificial elements to the scene's vanishing points and camera height.\n- **Particle Animation**: Simulate natural phenomena (smoke, fire, sparks, dust) using physics-driven particle systems.\n- **Procedural Animation**: Generate repetitive or complex movements algorithmically using expressions.\n- **Node-Based Compositing**: Build multi-layer visual effects using directed acyclic node graphs.\n- **Cleanup and Object Removal**: Remove booms, rigs, tracking markers, and blemishes via clean plates.\n- **Motion Interpolation**: Synthesize intermediate frames using optical flow algorithms.\n- **Temporal Consistency**: Prevent frame-to-frame flickering, edge chatter, and mask swimming.\n- **VFX Shot Continuity**: Ensure added effects match surrounding non-VFX footage in noise, contrast, and tone.\n- **Photorealistic Integration**: Match camera sensor noise, lens distortion, chromatic aberration, and grain.\n\n### 12. Typography, Graphics, and Caption Design\n- **Typographic Hierarchy**: Establish clear relative weight between headers, subheaders, and body text.\n- **Font Pairing**: Combine complementary typefaces (e.g. geometric sans with editorial serif).\n- **Kerning**: Adjust optical spacing between individual character pairs.\n- **Tracking**: Adjust overall character density across entire text strings.\n- **Leading**: Set vertical spacing between text lines to maintain reading ease.\n- **Alignment Consistency**: Maintain deliberate left, center, or right alignment grids.\n- **Grid-Based Layout**: Anchor all graphic elements to an underlying spatial grid.\n- **Contrast and Readability**: Ensure text maintains at least 4.5:1 luminance contrast against moving video.\n- **Text-Safe Areas**: Keep all critical text inside the inner 80% title-safe boundary.\n- **Kinetic Typography**: Animate text properties in rhythm with vocal cadence to drive comprehension.\n- **Word-by-Word Emphasis**: Highlight key power words selectively rather than animating every syllable.\n- **Caption Synchronization**: Lock caption display timing precisely to spoken phonemes.\n- **Caption Segmentation**: Break text into logical grammatical chunks (1-4 words per line).\n- **Speaker Identification**: Distinguish speakers using subtle color coding or name tags.\n- **Lower-Third Design**: Display names and locations in a clean, non-intrusive lower-third graphic.\n- **Infographic Animation**: Animate technical processes and quantitative metrics to facilitate understanding.\n- **Chart Animation**: Reveal data trends progressively along axes without misleading scaling.\n- **Brand Typography Consistency**: Enforce channel or corporate brand font guidelines strictly.\n- **Graphic Exit Timing**: Hold text on screen for at least 0.3 seconds per word plus 1 second base time before exiting.\n\n---\n\n## Part 4 — Platform, Commercial, and Genre Directing\n\n### 13. Short-Form and YouTube Editing\n- **Opening Hook**: Hook viewers within the first 0.5 to 3 seconds with a high-stakes premise or payoff teaser.\n- **First-Frame Clarity**: Ensure the opening frame is immediately visually comprehensible on small screens.\n- **Expectation Matching**: Fulfill the promise made in the title and thumbnail without bait-and-switch delay.\n- **Immediate Context**: Deliver essential setup without unnecessary background exposition.\n- **Curiosity Gap**: Open an unanswered question that demands watching until resolution.\n- **Delayed Payoff**: Build anticipation by postponing the primary resolution through progressive obstacles.\n- **Early Value Delivery**: Deliver a high-value insight or entertaining beat within the first 45 seconds.\n- **Dead-Air Reduction**: Trim pauses longer than 300ms using text-based or automated silence cuts.\n- **Visual Pattern Interruption**: Introduce visual changes (cuts, B-roll, punch-ins) every 5-7 seconds.\n- **Shot-Scale Variation**: Alternate between medium shots, close-ups, and punch-ins to maintain energy.\n- **B-Roll Illustration**: Illustrate spoken claims with relevant, concrete B-roll footage.\n- **Visual Proof**: Show data, screen captures, or experiments rather than relying solely on vocal claims.\n- **On-Screen Emphasis**: Reinforce critical statistics or keywords with dynamic on-screen callouts.\n- **Information Chunking**: Group complex arguments into numbered steps or distinct chapters.\n- **Progress Indicators**: Display chapter markers or progress bars to signal narrative momentum.\n- **Narrative Callbacks**: Revisit an early setup or joke toward the end of the video.\n- **Fast Context Recovery**: Reorient the viewer within 2 seconds whenever the topic shifts.\n- **Vertical-First Framing**: Compose subjects centrally within the 9:16 vertical crop.\n- **Mobile Legibility**: Ensure text, graphs, and fine details are legible on 6-inch mobile screens.\n- **Platform Interface Avoidance**: Keep text and subjects clear of TikTok/Reels UI buttons and overlays.\n- **Sound-Off Comprehension**: Ensure the core narrative is 100% understandable via captions alone.\n- **Platform-Native Style**: Match formatting, pacing, and tone to the specific platform culture.\n- **Loop-Friendly Endings**: Connect the final sentence to the opening hook for continuous repeat playback.\n- **Audience-Retention Analysis**: Identify specific timestamps where retention dips occur.\n- **Retention-Based Revision**: Tighten sections that show viewer drop-off in post-release iterations.\n- **Thumbnail-to-Video Consistency**: Match the color palette, subject, and framing of the thumbnail in the first 5 seconds.\n- **Ending and Next-Step Clarity**: Conclude decisively without drawn-out outros; direct immediately to the next video.\n\n### 14. Commercial, Brand, and Product Editing\n- **Brand-Message Clarity**: Crystallize the primary value proposition within the opening 10 seconds.\n- **Product-First Storytelling**: Position the product as the hero that resolves the customer's problem.\n- **Problem-Solution Structure**: Depict the acute pain point before introducing the solution.\n- **Benefit-Led Editing**: Emphasize tangible user benefits rather than purely technical feature lists.\n- **Product Demonstration**: Show the product in active, practical use under authentic conditions.\n- **Premium Visual Restraint**: Maintain high aesthetic quality by eliminating unnecessary visual clutter.\n- **Brand Color Consistency**: Enforce strict corporate brand color standards across graphics and grades.\n- **Brand Motion Language**: Standardize transition styles, animation ease curves, and graphic layouts.\n- **Visual Proof of Claims**: Back performance claims with verified side-by-side comparisons or certifications.\n- **Testimonial Pacing**: Preserve authentic conversational pauses to protect customer credibility.\n- **Social-Proof Integration**: Incorporate verifiable customer reviews, awards, and adoption metrics.\n- **Call-to-Action Timing**: Present the call to action clearly with sufficient screen duration.\n- **Creative Variant Testing**: Produce multiple hook and pacing cuts for algorithmic A/B testing.\n- **Format Versioning**: Cut distinct aspect ratios (16:9, 9:16, 1:1, 4:5) and durations (6s, 15s, 30s, 60s).\n\n### 15. Documentary, Interview, and Nonfiction Editing\n- **Truthful Representation**: Cut footage without distorting the substantive meaning of participants' statements.\n- **Interview Logging**: Transcribe and catalog all interviews with descriptive topic tags and timecodes.\n- **Paper Editing**: Construct the narrative backbone from transcript selections prior to timeline cutting.\n- **Theme-Based Assembly**: Group documentary footage around conceptual themes rather than raw capture order.\n- **Character-Driven Narrative**: Anchor institutional or historical topics through personal human experiences.\n- **Context Preservation**: Retain sufficient conversational context so quotes cannot be misinterpreted.\n- **Archival Integration**: Blend archival footage, photographs, and historical documents cleanly.\n- **Source Attribution**: Label all archival sources, interviewees, and third-party footage clearly on screen.\n- **Chronological Clarity**: Guide the viewer through time jumps using dates, maps, or distinct color cues.\n- **Expository Editing**: Structure facts, evidence, and narration to build an analytical argument.\n- **Observational Editing**: Preserve continuous real-time events with minimal editorial interference.\n- **Participatory Storytelling**: Transparently disclose the filmmaker's presence and interaction with subjects.\n- **Contrasting Perspectives**: Juxtapose opposing viewpoints fairly to present balanced nuance.\n- **Fair Interview Assembly**: Avoid splicing answers to different questions together into misleading statements.\n- **Evidence Sequencing**: Introduce supporting facts in a progressive order that mirrors logical deduction.\n- **Reconstruction Labeling**: Clearly label simulated or reenacted scenes to distinguish from authentic footage.\n- **Sensitive-Subject Handling**: Protect the privacy, safety, and consent of vulnerable subjects.\n- **Ethical Emotional Storytelling**: Elicit genuine emotional resonance without sensationalizing human suffering.\n\n---\n\n## Part 5 — Elite Production Workflow and Delivery\n\n### 16. Professional Editing Workflow and Collaboration\n- **Editorial Brief**: Clarify deliverables, aspect ratios, target audience, and stylistic references before cutting.\n- **Reference Research**: Review benchmark videos to identify genre-specific pacing, grading, and sound conventions.\n- **Storyboard Planning**: Pre-visualize shot coverage and transition mechanics.\n- **Shot-List Planning**: Verify that all required master shots, close-ups, and cutaways were captured.\n- **Media Ingestion**: Copy media from camera cards to storage using verified checksum utilities.\n- **Checksum Verification**: Validate bit-for-bit file integrity using MD5, SHA-256, or xxHash.\n- **Backup Strategy**: Implement the 3-2-1 backup rule (3 copies, 2 media types, 1 offsite).\n- **Folder Organization**: Maintain standardized project directory structures across all projects.\n- **Naming Conventions**: Apply consistent file naming (`YYMMDD_PROJECT_SCENE_TAKE_VERSION`).\n- **Metadata Management**: Preserve camera reel, timecode, lens data, and scene numbers.\n- **Proxy Workflow**: Generate lightweight editing proxies (ProRes Proxy, DNxHR LB) linked to camera originals.\n- **Multicamera Synchronization**: Align multi-camera feeds using timecode or scratch audio waveforms.\n- **Footage Logging**: Flag preferred takes, identify usable soundbites, and record technical defects.\n- **Select Reels / Stringouts**: Pull all usable takes into thematic stringout sequences.\n- **Radio Edit**: Assemble the complete spoken narrative spine before cutting picture.\n- **Assembly Cut**: Lay out all scenes in script order without fine trimming.\n- **Rough Cut**: Refine narrative structure, identify plot holes, and eliminate redundant scenes.\n- **Fine Cut**: Polish performance timing, micro-rhythms, dialogue overlaps, and shot selections.\n- **Picture Lock**: Freeze all edit points before handing off to color, VFX, and audio finishing.\n- **VFX Turnover**: Export high-resolution plate clips with handles (usually 8-24 frames) and EDLs/XMLs.\n- **Sound Turnover**: Export AAF/OMF audio sessions with handles for Fairlight/Pro Tools mixing.\n- **Color Turnover**: Export XML/EDL conforms linked to camera raw files for grading in DaVinci Resolve.\n- **Timeline Interchange**: Exchange sequences between NLEs via standardized XML, EDL, or OTIO formats.\n- **Conforming**: Re-link the fine cut timeline to original camera raw files for final render.\n- **Version Control**: Save incremental project versions at every milestone (`_v01`, `_v02`, `_rc1`, `_locked`).\n- **Collaborative Review**: Gather timecode-specific stakeholder notes using Frame.io or similar platforms.\n- **Revision Tracking**: Systematically address and check off each review note before output.\n- **Final Project Archiving**: Archive project files, master exports, and final assets into cold storage.\n\n### 17. Export, Mastering, and Quality-Control Principles\n- **Delivery Specification Compliance**: Adhere strictly to resolution, frame rate, and codec guidelines.\n- **Aspect-Ratio Management**: Ensure letterboxing or pillarboxing matches delivery mandates.\n- **Native Frame-Rate Preservation**: Avoid unintended frame rate conversions that cause judder.\n- **Mixed-Frame-Rate Handling**: Convert mixed footage using optical flow or motion interpolation where needed.\n- **Motion-Cadence Checking**: Inspect panning and high-speed motion for cadence stutter or ghosting.\n- **Resolution Management**: Deliver native resolution without unnecessary upscaling artifacts.\n- **Pixel-Aspect-Ratio Accuracy**: Enforce square pixels (1.0) to prevent geometric stretching.\n- **Container Selection**: Select MP4, MOV, or MXF wrappers appropriate for destination platforms.\n- **Codec Selection**: Choose delivery codecs (H.264/H.265 for web, ProRes 422 HQ / DNxHR for archive).\n- **Bit-Depth and Chroma Management**: Preserve 10-bit 4:2:2 color depth on master deliverables.\n- **Bitrate Optimization**: Target optimal bitrates (4K: 45-65 Mbps; 1080p: 12-20 Mbps for H.264).\n- **GOP Configuration**: Use closed GOP structure with keyframe interval matching the frame rate.\n- **Audio Format Selection**: Deliver uncompressed PCM (24-bit, 48kHz) or high-bitrate AAC (384 kbps).\n- **Timecode Accuracy**: Ensure timeline timecode tracks match master delivery specifications.\n- **Audio-Channel Mapping**: Route stereo (Ch 1-2) or surround 5.1/7.1 tracks to designated channels.\n- **Color-Transform Verification**: Verify output color space tags (Rec.709, sRGB) match rendered footage.\n- **Color-Metadata Validation**: Confirm NCLC tags (`1-1-1` for Rec.709) prevent gamma shift on Apple devices.\n- **HDR-to-SDR Conversion**: Apply tone-mapping transforms to ensure HDR masters display correctly in SDR.\n- **Dynamic-Range Preservation**: Prevent highlight clipping and crushed shadows during compression.\n- **Legal-Signal Checking**: Ensure video signal stays within legal broadcast limits (16-235 for 8-bit).\n- **Audio Peak Checking**: Verify true peaks do not exceed -1.0 dBTP to avoid inter-sample clipping.\n- **Lip-Sync Validation**: Confirm mouth movements match spoken audio with zero-frame latency.\n- **Full-Length Playback Review**: Watch the entire exported master in real time before client delivery.\n- **Caption-File Validation**: Verify SRT/VTT caption timing, formatting, and character encoding.\n- **Language-Version Verification**: Check alternate audio dubs and localized subtitles for correct alignment.\n- **Cross-Device Testing**: Preview the final deliverable across mobile, desktop, TV, and studio monitors.\n\n### 18. Accessibility, Legal, and Ethical Principles\n- **Accurate Captions**: Transcribe dialogue verbatim with high punctuation accuracy.\n- **Caption Timing**: Sync subtitle display with speech, avoiding early reveals that spoil jokes.\n- **Non-Speech Captions**: Include descriptive text for important sound effects, music changes, and atmosphere.\n- **Accessible Transcripts**: Provide full text transcripts alongside video releases.\n- **Audio Description**: Provide spoken narration of visual action for blind or visually impaired viewers.\n- **Readable Text Contrast**: Maintain WCAG AA compliant contrast (minimum 4.5:1) for all on-screen graphics.\n- **Accessible Motion Design**: Avoid high-frequency flashes or disorienting camera movements.\n- **Photosensitivity Safety**: Avoid strobe effects that exceed 3 flashes per second to prevent seizures.\n- **Reasonable Reading Duration**: Ensure on-screen text remains visible long enough for comfortable reading.\n- **Speech-to-Background Balance**: Maintain at least 15dB separation between dialogue and background music.\n- **Localization Quality**: Translate cultural nuances and idioms faithfully in foreign-language releases.\n- **Accessible Data Presentation**: Complement color-coded charts with patterns, shapes, or text labels.\n- **Informed Consent**: Verify signed release forms from all on-screen participants before publication.\n- **Privacy Protection**: Blur license plates, private addresses, and unintended bystander faces.\n- **Sensitive-Subject Safeguards**: Handle trauma and violence with editorial restraint and trigger advisories.\n- **Footage Licensing**: Clear commercial licenses for all stock footage, archival clips, and third-party media.\n- **Trademark and Brand Compliance**: Ensure third-party logos do not violate fair use or cause trademark infringement.\n- **Music and Sound Licensing**: Obtain synchronization and master use rights for all soundtrack assets.\n- **AI-Content Transparency**: Disclose AI-generated or synthetic footage in compliance with platform rules.\n- **Truthful Editing**: Refuse to edit sequences in ways that manufacture false claims or distort real events.\n\n---\n\n## Part 6 — AI-Assisted Editing and Editorial Mastery\n\n### 19. Modern AI Editing Techniques\n- **AI Footage Indexing**: Auto-tag and organize media libraries by detected subjects, scenes, and actions.\n- **Automatic Transcription**: Convert spoken dialogue to time-coded text transcripts with speaker labels.\n- **Text-Based Editing**: Edit video sequences by selecting, cutting, and rearranging text in transcripts.\n- **AI-Assisted Paper Editing**: Generate rough assemblies from selected transcript passages automatically.\n- **Silence Detection**: Flag pauses and dead air for automated or review-based removal.\n- **Multilingual Transcription**: Generate transcripts across multiple languages with language identification.\n- **Speaker Diarization**: Automatically differentiate between distinct voices in a multi-speaker recording.\n- **Semantic Footage Search**: Retrieve specific shots by natural language descriptions (\"medium shot of woman smiling\").\n- **Automatic Metadata Tagging**: Tag clips by emotion, shot size, camera angle, and scene setting.\n- **Intelligent Reframing**: Dynamically track subjects when cropping 16:9 footage to 9:16 vertical.\n- **AI Background Isolation**: Separate subjects from backgrounds without green screens.\n- **Assisted Masking and Tracking**: Generate rotoscope masks automatically across moving footage.\n- **AI Noise Reduction**: Remove ambient background noise using neural audio isolation models.\n- **AI Speech Enhancement**: Reconstruct low-quality or clipped voice recordings to studio quality.\n- **Automatic Caption Generation**: Draft synchronized open and closed captions with punctuation.\n- **Assisted Subtitle Translation**: Translate subtitles into foreign languages for human review.\n- **Automated Shot Matching**: Balance color temperature and exposure between adjacent clips algorithmically.\n- **Scene and Shot Detection**: Detect cut points in pre-rendered video files and slice into editable segments.\n- **Generative Sound Design**: Synthesize custom sound effects and ambient beds from text descriptions.\n- **Generative Video Assets**: Produce supplementary B-roll and visual cutaways using models like Google Veo 3.1.\n- **Generative Clip Extension**: Extend the head or tail of a short shot to resolve editing timing deficits.\n- **AI-Output Validation**: Audit synthetic media for physical artifacts, warping, and temporal flickering.\n\n### 20. Advanced Editorial Judgment and Mastery\n- **Edit Without Music First**: Ensure the visual pacing and performance work independently before adding score.\n- **Listen Without Watching**: Play the timeline with the monitor off to evaluate audio rhythm and clarity.\n- **Watch Without Sound**: Play the timeline muted to verify that visual storytelling communicates clearly.\n- **Full-Screen Review**: Watch exports full-screen away from timeline software to experience as a viewer.\n- **Real-Device Review**: Review the cut on an actual smartphone, laptop, and television before delivery.\n- **Fresh-Eyes Review**: Step away for at least two hours before conducting final polish passes.\n- **A/B Cut Comparison**: Maintain two candidate cuts of difficult scenes and compare them back to back.\n- **Audience Feedback Analysis**: Track viewer confusion, boredom, or laughter to locate pacing errors.\n- **Cut-Purpose Questioning**: Challenge every cut by asking what specific value it adds to the scene.\n- **Redundancy Removal**: Cut any shot, phrase, or effect that repeats previously established information.\n- **Reaction Preservation**: Guard authentic emotional reactions from being trimmed for mere timing efficiency.\n- **Creative Decision Documentation**: Record the artistic rationale behind non-obvious editorial choices.\n- **Deliberate Editorial Practice**: Periodically re-cut existing material to test alternative narrative strategies.\n\n---\n\n## Format Priority Matrix\n\n| Format | Top Editorial Priorities | Recommended Shot Length | Audio Target |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Hollywood Feature** | Emotion, performance selection, spatial continuity, subtext | 3.0s – 8.0s | -24 LUFS / 5.1 |\n| **YouTube Long-Form** | Packaging match, early payoff, pattern interrupts, open loops | 2.5s – 5.0s | -14 LUFS / Stereo |\n| **Reels / Shorts / TikTok** | 0.5s pre-hook, mobile legibility, sound-off captions, loop ending | 1.0s – 2.5s | -14 LUFS / Mobile EQ |\n| **Commercials (15-30s)** | Brand clarity, problem-solution, benefit demonstration, CTA | 1.0s – 2.0s | -24 or -14 LUFS |\n| **Documentary** | Truthful context, interview clarity, evidence progression, ethics | 4.0s – 12.0s | -24 LUFS |\n| **Music Video** | Musical phrase sync, syncopated cuts, energy matching, style | 0.5s – 2.0s | -14 LUFS |\n\n---\n\n## Mastery Progression Order\n\nDevelop editorial capabilities in this structured sequence:\n1. Emotion and Storytelling\n2. Cut Timing and Pacing\n3. Continuity and Visual Composition\n4. Dialogue and Sound Editing\n5. Shot Selection and Performance\n6. Color Correction and Shot Matching\n7. Transitions and Montage\n8. Music and Sound Design\n9. Typography and Motion Graphics\n10. Professional Workflow and Quality Control\n11. Format-Specific Editing and Audience Analysis\n12. AI-Assisted Efficiency and Creative Human Judgment\n\n---\n\n## Self-Improvement Protocol (Autonomous Skill Evolution)\n\nThis skill improves dynamically whenever new corrections, trends, or user feedback arrive.\n\n### Correction Ingestion Procedure\n1. **Log Correction**: Append entry to `.agents/state/corrections/CORRECTIONS_LOG.md` with timestamp, principle section, before text, after text, and category.\n2. **Apply Update**: Edit `video-editing-principles/SKILL.md` directly.\n3. **Preserve Test Ratchet**: Total principles must never decrease without explicit written justification.\n4. **Bump Version**: Increment `metadata.version` patch level.\n5. **Rebuild Manifest**: Execute `python3 scripts/build_tiered_skills_manifest.py`.\n6. **Cross-Update Dependents**: Update references in [google-flow](../../google-flow/SKILL.md), [youtube-long-form-production](../../youtube-long-form-production/SKILL.md), and [ffmpeg-video-editor](../../ffmpeg-video-editor/SKILL.md).\n\n---\n\n## Related AGYOS Skills\n\n- [google-flow](../../google-flow/SKILL.md) — Headless Veo 3.1 video generation and Scene Builder.\n- [veo-3](../../veo-3/SKILL.md) — Director prompt anatomy for Google Veo.\n- [ai-video](../../ai-video/SKILL.md) — Model router and brief architecture.\n- [ffmpeg-video-editor](../../ffmpeg-video-editor/SKILL.md) — Automated shell-based timeline assembly and audio mastering.\n- [youtube-long-form-production](../../youtube-long-form-production/SKILL.md) — 4-act retention scripting and packaging pre-production.\n- [video-editing](../../video-editing/SKILL.md) — Core editing workflow and Remotion composition.\n- [agyos-self-improvement](../../agyos-self-improvement/SKILL.md) — Catalog-wide autonomous self-correction engine.\n"
